### How it works

#### The network

- Transactions: users sign transactions spending unspent transaction outputs (UTXOs) with their private keys and broadcast them to peer-to-peer nodes. Transactions wait in each node's mempool and are prioritised by fee rate (sat/vB).

- Mining (proof-of-work): miners assemble blocks (≤4M weight units, ~1–4 MB) and search for a SHA-256d hash below the target. The winning miner's coinbase transaction may claim subsidy + all fees in the block. Target block time is 10 minutes; difficulty retargets every 2,016 blocks. Current difficulty 148.36T (all-time high 155.97T at block 921,312, 2025-10-29); the next retarget at block 975,744 (~2026-11-13) is estimated at -6.92% (re-check 15:35 UTC; -7.16% at 14:30 UTC) because recent blocks have averaged ~10.75 min (645.0 s) ([mempool.space](https://mempool.space/api/v1/difficulty-adjustment)).

- Hashrate: 30-day average ~1,029 EH/s (range 825–1,228 EH/s daily); current estimate ~964 EH/s (15:35 UTC; ~961 EH/s at 14:30 UTC); record ~1,306 EH/s daily average on 2025-10-25 ([mempool.space hashrate](https://mempool.space/api/v1/mining/hashrate/1m)).

- Nodes: anyone can run a full node that validates every rule; consensus rules are enforced by node software (dominantly Bitcoin Core; Bitcoin Knots is the main alternative: 5,429 of 27,148 reachable nodes (20.00%) on 2026-08-14 20:17 UTC per [CleanSky](https://cleansky.io/blog/bip-110-nodes-vs-hashrate-governance-2026/); reachable-node counts exclude non-listening nodes). Full chain size on disk: ~891.7 GB (891,656,583,179 bytes, publicnode getblockchaininfo, 2026-11-10 15:35 UTC).

- Governance: there is no on-chain governance, token vote, foundation or treasury. Rule changes happen through BIPs (Bitcoin Improvement Proposals), rough consensus among developers, then voluntary adoption by node operators (economic majority) with miner signalling for soft forks. The 2026 BIP-110 episode (see News) shows that a soft fork with little miner support and mandatory enforcement splits its supporters off rather than changing Bitcoin.

#### Token mechanics (what does and does not exist)

| Mechanism | Exists for BTC? | Notes |

| --- | --- | --- |

| Gas / fee token | Yes | Fees are paid in BTC to the miner of the block; there is no base-fee burn. |

| Staking / yield | No (native) | No protocol staking. "BTC yield" products (Babylon BTC staking, lending, ETF covered calls, Lightning routing) are third-party layers with their own risk. |

| Fee switch / buyback / burn | No | Nothing is bought back or burned. Coins sent to provably unspendable outputs (OP_RETURN, lost keys) reduce effective supply but are not a mechanism. |

| Governance voting | No | Holding BTC confers no vote. |

| Collateral | Off-protocol | Widely used as collateral at CeFi lenders, CME futures, DeFi (via wrapped BTC), and corporate preferred-stock structures (Strategy's STRC/STRF/STRK/STRD). |

| Supply cap / halving | Yes | The defining mechanic: fixed, predictable, declining issuance. |

#### Layers and products built on top

- Lightning Network (payment channels): 16,242 public nodes, 32,678 channels, 3,795.5 BTC public capacity (mempool.space "latest" snapshot, still dated 2026-08-30 on 2026-11-10, i.e. the API is stale by ~10 weeks; the earlier read returned 16,237 / 32,674 / 3,794.5 BTC for the same date) ([source](https://mempool.space/api/v1/lightning/statistics/latest)).

- Custodial wrappers and access products: spot ETFs (US since Jan 2024), exchange balances, wrapped BTC on EVM chains and Solana.

- Data/inscriptions: Ordinals/inscriptions and Runes use witness and OP_RETURN space; Bitcoin Core v30 (Oct 2025) relaxed the default OP_RETURN relay limit, which triggered the Core-vs-Knots dispute.

### Utility

- Real, protocol-level utility: (1) the only asset that can pay Bitcoin transaction fees; (2) a bearer asset settled on a neutral, permissionless network with ~16 years of uptime and ~1 ZH/s of hashrate securing it; (3) self-custody with no issuer or counterparty.

- Market-level utility: the reserve/benchmark asset of the crypto market (59.75% dominance, 2026-11-10), the main collateral and quote asset for crypto derivatives, a treasury reserve asset for ~197 listed companies, and an ETF-wrapped macro asset in traditional portfolios.

- Payments: niche. ~580,000 on-chain transactions/day (last 144 blocks: 580,178 tx at 14:30 UTC; 565,185 tx at 15:35 UTC) and Lightning capacity of ~3,800 BTC show that BTC is used far more as a store of value/investment than as a medium of exchange.

- What BTC is NOT needed for: governance (no votes), earning protocol cash flows (none exist), staking for security (security comes from external energy/hardware), or using most DeFi (wrapped versions or other chains are used instead).

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- Scarcity / "digital gold" thesis: fixed 21M cap, ~0.82% annual inflation falling to ~0.4% after the 2028 halving.

- Institutional wrappers: US spot ETFs held ~1.29M BTC (Oct 2026, single secondary report, unverified; IBIT alone ≈795,900 BTC on 2026-11-09 per iShares). ETF flows are the dominant marginal buyer: in Q3 2026 ETFs added 79,800 BTC ($6.2B), nearly 2× the 41,300 BTC mined, after $4.8B of outflows in Q2 ([21shares](https://www.21shares.com/en-us/insights/bitcoin-etf-inflows-q3-2026)). August 2026 was the best ETF month of 2026 (+$3.52B, BTC +~25%) ([Cointelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/bitcoin-etf-best-month-2026-btc-up-25-august)).

- Corporate treasuries: Strategy and ~196 other companies; Strategy bought $14.33B of BTC YTD 2026 (to 2026-10-25), funded by MSTR stock and preferred issuance, while also selling ~$431.8M of BTC YTD to fund preferred dividends and STRC buybacks ([Q3 release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001050446/000105044626000047/mstr-20261029x8kxex9912.htm)).

- Sovereign/policy narratives: US Strategic Bitcoin Reserve (executive order March 2025; no purchases), state reserves (e.g. Texas), Russia opening licensed retail access.

- Macro/liquidity: BTC trades as a high-beta risk/liquidity asset. Example: on 2026-11-05 BTC fell up to 5.8% to below $76,000 alongside a 6.4% Nasdaq-100 drop ([Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-11-05/bitcoin-sinks-to-seven-week-low-as-risk-off-mood-deepens)).

- Halving-cycle reflexivity: many participants trade the ~4-year cycle (peak Oct 2025 ~18 months after the April 2024 halving).

#### Supply / sell pressure

- Miner selling: ~450 BTC/day new issuance (~$33M/day); miners sell a large share to cover power. Hashprice ~$34.8/PH/s/day (own estimate, see Revenue); October 2026 average $37.92 and $32.96 on 2026-10-31 ([SimpleMining](https://www.simplemining.io/insights/post/bitcoin-market-report-october-2026)). Earlier-2026 lows ($28–30 in Q1, $30.37 June average, Luxor-cited) are from secondary reports and were not re-verified.

- Long-term holders / early whales: LTH balances fell by 248,000 BTC ($17.4B) in Q3 2026, ~3× ETF purchases ([21shares/Glassnode](https://www.21shares.com/en-us/insights/bitcoin-etf-inflows-q3-2026)).

- ETF outflows: October 2026 ≈ -$515M net; Nov 2–6 ≈ -$1.28B net (secondary trackers; figures differ by tracker).

- Treasury-company stress: Strategy sold ~$431.8M of BTC in 2026 to 2026-10-25 (primary: [Q3 release](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001050446/000105044626000047/mstr-20261029x8kxex9912.htm)), including 3,588 BTC for $216M disclosed in an 8-K on 2026-07-06 (largest BTC disposal in its history) and 5,553 BTC ($349M) in July–August in total; smaller reported sales (32 BTC in late May; 1,638 BTC in late July at ~$63,957) are secondary and were not re-verified ([21shares](https://www.21shares.com/en-us/insights/bitcoin-etf-inflows-q3-2026), [Bitcoin Magazine](https://bitcoinmagazine.com/news/strategy-mstr-sells-3588-bitcoin)). 16 of the 20 largest digital-asset treasury companies traded below NAV in late Sept 2026 (DWF Ventures, secondary).

- Estates/legacy supply: Mt. Gox (~34.4k BTC, deadline 2027-10-31), government seizure sales (US says it will not sell SBR coins; other governments have sold).

- Leverage: liquidations in perpetual futures amplify moves both ways.

### The protocol/business

Bitcoin is a monetary network, not a company. Its "business" is final settlement of BTC transfers, paid for by users via fees and by all holders via dilution (subsidy). Its "customers" are holders, exchanges, custodians, ETFs, miners and payment users.

- Where it is popular: US institutions (ETFs, listed companies), global exchanges (Binance, Coinbase etc.), emerging markets for savings/remittances, and state-level experiments (El Salvador; US federal/state reserves). Russia: law signed 2026-08-04 legalises regulated crypto trading; the central bank approved BTC/ETH/USDT for exchange trading with retail limits (~300,000 RUB per intermediary per year) ([Meduza](https://meduza.io/en/news/2026/08/11/russia-s-central-bank-approves-bitcoin-ethereum-and-usdt-for-exchange-trading-with-limits-for-retail-investors)).

- Mining pools (last 7 days, 915 blocks, re-check 2026-11-10 15:35 UTC): Foundry USA 25.0%, AntPool 19.8%, F2Pool 16.1%, ViaBTC 9.3%, SpiderPool 6.9%, MARA Pool 5.4%, OCEAN 5.2%, SECPOOL 4.9%, Luxor 3.8%, Braiins 2.0% ([mempool.space pools/1w](https://mempool.space/api/v1/mining/pools/1w)). The top 2 pools found ~45% and the top 3 ~61% of blocks. Binance Pool is discontinued on 2026-11-10 per a notice dated 2026-10-12 ([Bitcoin.com](https://news.bitcoin.com/binance-pulls-plug-bitcoin-mining-after-6-year-run/)).

- Mining industry shift: public miners are moving capacity to AI/HPC hosting; a CryptoQuant report says five public miners (IREN, Core Scientific, Bitfarms, TeraWulf, Cipher) produced 7,075 BTC from 2026-01-01 to 2026-10-19 vs 13,263 BTC in the same period of 2025 (-47%) (secondary, [Market Periodical](https://themarketperiodical.com/2026/10/23/bitcoin-mining-output-falls-47/)).

