- Beacon deposit contract 0x00000000219ab540356cBB839Cbe05303d7705Fa: 93,255,698 ETH (15:4x UTC read). This is cumulative deposits ever made. The contract never pays out, because withdrawals are minted on the consensus layer, so this number is not live stake.

- BitMine Immersion (NYSE: BMNR): 6,070,092 ETH, about 4.97% of supply (reported 2026-11-09) [17]. BitMine reported 5,227,037 ETH of it (≈86%) as staked in the same 2026-11-09 disclosure [17].

- US spot ETFs: $12.07B net assets on 2026-11-09 per SoSoValue [17], about 5.9M ETH at $2,061 (estimate derived from that figure).

- Lido: about $20.1B TVL on Ethereum, ≈9.8M ETH, ≈22% of all stake (DefiLlama, own calculation) [11].

- WETH contract: 2.10M ETH.

- Ethereum Foundation main wallet 0xde0B295669a9FD93d5F28D9Ec85E40f4cb697BAe: 4,774.55 ETH (own read). The EF also stakes about 70,000 ETH [21]. The EF's total treasury across all wallets was not available from a primary source in this session.

### How it works

#### The product for users

Ethereum is a public, permissionless computer. Users sign transactions that transfer ETH or tokens or call smart contracts (DEXs, lending markets, stablecoins, NFTs, bridges, rollup contracts). Every operation consumes gas, which is paid in ETH. A new block arrives every 12 seconds. Blocks are final after two epochs, about 12.8 minutes. Since the 2022 Merge, security comes from proof of stake (validators bond ETH) rather than mining.

#### Fee market (EIP-1559 + blobs)

- Each block has a protocol-set base fee per gas that adjusts by up to ±12.5% a block toward a 50% gas target. The base fee is burned (destroyed). Users can add a priority fee (tip), which goes to the block proposer.

- The block gas limit is 60M gas, so the target is 30M. Validators signalled it to 60M around 2025-11-25, ahead of Fusaka [13]. Fusaka also capped a single transaction at 16,777,216 gas (EIP-7825) [12].

- Blobs (EIP-4844, March 2024): rollups post data in "blobs", which have their own base fee that is also burned. Fusaka (mainnet 2025-12-03) added PeerDAS. The "blob-parameter-only" forks raised the per-block blob target/max to 10/15 (BPO1, 2025-12-09) and then to 14/21 (BPO2, 2026-01-07) [12][13]. In the hours sampled, blocks carried 9.92 (14:3x UTC) and 9.79 (15:4x UTC) blobs on average, below the 14 target. The blob base fee is therefore very low: 0.131 gwei per blob gas at 15:41 UTC (eth_blobBaseFee), i.e. ≈0.0000172 ETH (≈$0.035) per 131,072-gas blob, and blob burn was 0.008–0.017 ETH per hour (own reads, section 8).

- Current prices: base fee 0.37 gwei at block 26,385,129 (14:3x UTC) and 2.30 gwei at block 26,385,476 (15:4x UTC) (own RPC reads; the base fee is spiky). The average over the last 1,024 blocks was 0.141 gwei at 14:3x UTC and 0.345 gwei at 15:4x UTC, with blocks 51–52% full (eth_feeHistory, own reads). A simple transfer costs fractions of a cent.

#### Staking (consensus)

- Validators deposit at least 32 ETH. Since Pectra (May 2025) a validator can hold up to 2,048 ETH effective balance if it uses 0x02 "compounding" credentials (EIP-7251) [21]. This is why the validator count fell from about 976k (Feb 2026) to 814k while total stake rose [5].

- Rewards come from three sources:

- new issuance for attestations, proposals and sync committees;

- priority fees;

- MEV, paid by block builders via MEV-Boost relays.

Penalties apply for downtime, and slashing applies for equivocation.

- Entry and exit are rate-limited by a "churn" of 256 ETH per epoch for each direction (validatorqueue data) [5]. On 2026-11-09 the entry queue was 903,754 ETH with a 16.2-day wait, and the exit queue was 5,061 ETH [5].

- About 90% of blocks are built by external builders through MEV-Boost. Over the last 7 days, Titan built 52.1% of relayed blocks, Quasar 17.7% and BuilderNet 15.2%. Relays: ultrasound 33.8%, Titan 32.4%, Aestus 19.5%, bloXroute regulated 12.3% (relayscan.io, 2026-11-10) [14].

#### Governance

Ethereum has no on-chain token vote. Protocol changes are EIPs. They are debated and scheduled on the public All Core Devs calls (ACDE for execution, ACDC for consensus) and shipped as hard forks that node operators choose to run. ETH holders have no formal vote. The EF coordinates and funds this work but does not control it. There is no treasury fee switch, protocol buyback or "DAO treasury". The only protocol-level value mechanisms are burn (all ETH holders) and issuance + tips + MEV (stakers).

#### Roadmap context

- Recent forks: Dencun (2024-03, blobs); Pectra (2025-05, larger validators, EIP-7702 smart accounts); Fusaka (2025-12-03, PeerDAS, gas cap, BPO forks) [12].

- Next, Glamsterdam:

- enshrined PBS (EIP-7732);

- block-level access lists (EIP-7928);

- gas and state repricing (EIP-8037/8038).

It also raises exit and consolidation churn (EIP-8061) and removes the SELFDESTRUCT burn (EIP-8246). Its Hoodi testnet plan included a 200M gas limit [7][8]. It went live on Sepolia on 2026-10-06. Hoodi was postponed and mainnet is unscheduled (section 10).

- After that comes Hegotá, with 62 candidate EIPs under scoping; FOCIL (fork-choice enforced inclusion lists) is slated as the consensus-layer headliner [28]. The EF Protocol cluster targets post-quantum security across all layers by December 2029 [10].

### Utility

- Real, enforced utility:

- Gas. Every L1 transaction must pay its fee in ETH, and so must most L2s, including Base, Arbitrum and OP Mainnet.

- Staking. Only ETH can secure consensus and earn issuance, tips and MEV.

- Collateral and money. ETH, WETH and LSTs are the base collateral of Aave ($12.5B Ethereum TVL, 2026-11-10), Morpho, Sky/Maker and Lido/EigenLayer restaking [11], and the main quote asset in on-chain trading.

- Settlement asset for L2s. Rollups pay L1 fees for blobs and for state roots and proofs, in ETH.

- What ETH is NOT needed for:

- Governance. There is no token vote.

- Holding stablecoins or tokenized assets themselves. USDT and USDC can be used on Ethereum with ETH only for gas, and gas can be sponsored by paymasters or EIP-7702 relayers.

- Value capture from L2 activity beyond blob and L1 fees. L2s paid only about $83.6k to L1 in the last 30 days (growthepie "rent paid", own aggregation) [15].

- Utility versus value: utility demand for ETH as gas is now tiny in dollar terms: $20.05M of total fees in 30 days on a ≈$249B asset (2026-11-10 15:39 UTC). Most of the holding motive is monetary (collateral, store of value, staking yield) and speculative (section 5). Vitalik Buterin made the same point explicitly on 2026-11-08 [19].

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- "Digital oil / store of value / reserve collateral" narrative. The investment thesis now pushed by Buterin is that ETH's value comes from decentralization and "CROPS" (censorship resistance, open source, privacy, security), not from burn. He wrote that fee burn could stay "< 10% of breakeven indefinitely" [19].

- Institutional wrappers. US spot ETFs launched in July 2024. Staking ETFs followed, including BlackRock's ETHB, listed on Nasdaq on 2026-03-12, which passes 82% of staking rewards to holders and reportedly stakes 70–95% of holdings (the staking ratio was not confirmed by the fact-checker) [20]. Combined US spot ETF net assets were $12.07B and cumulative net inflow $11.74B on 2026-11-09 (SoSoValue via [17]).

- Corporate treasuries. BitMine (chairman Tom Lee) went from about 3.4M ETH (Nov 2025) to 6.07M ETH (Nov 2026) [17][18]. Smaller "ETH treasury" companies (SharpLink and others) exist, and some, such as FG Nexus (10,922 ETH sold, reported 2026-11-03), have been sellers in 2026 [18].

- Staking yield. 2.46% APR, ETH-denominated [5]. Large staking demand persists: the entry queue has exceeded 0.9M ETH every day since 2026-01-02 and peaked at 4.10M ETH on 2026-02-12 (70-day wait at the 2026-02-01 reading of 4.04M) [5]. This queue removes liquid supply.

- Ecosystem dominance. Ethereum has about 53% of all DeFi TVL and 46% of stablecoins (DefiLlama, 2026-11-10; section 6). Tokenized RWAs and stablecoin issuers chose it as their primary chain.

- Speculation and beta. ETH is the second-largest crypto asset and the default "alt-beta" leg. It has deep perp, options and CME markets.

#### Supply and sell pressure

- Issuance: about 1.09M ETH a year is minted to stakers, roughly $2.2B a year at the current price [3]. Professional stakers (exchanges, LST operators, BitMine's MAVAN) sell part of it to cover costs. BitMine said it may sell staking rewards so it stays at or below 5% of supply [18].

- Weak burn: burn is only 2,727 ETH per 30 days, so it no longer offsets issuance. Annual burn in dollars fell from $5.14B (2021) to $285M (2025) and $43M in 2026 YTD (DefiLlama calendar sums) [6].

- ETF redemptions: October 2026 saw more than $1B of outflows and a record 23-day outflow streak [16]. Outflows were $365.2M over 2–6 November and $184.5M on 2026-11-09 [17].

- End of the marginal buyer: on about 2026-10-07 at TOKEN2049, BitMine said 5% of supply is a "hard cap" [18]. At 6.07M ETH it is about 40k ETH from 5% of 122.2M.

- Ecosystem budget sales: the EF periodically sells ETH to fund operations. One example is 1,000 ETH (≈$2.1M) sold on 2026-11-10 via an EF-linked wallet per Lookonchain, as cited by [17]. A prior sale was 2,785 ETH (≈$6.4M) swapped to USDS on CoW Swap, reported 2026-10-27 [18b]. The figures are small compared with ETF flows.

- Leverage: about $462M of liquidations occurred on 2026-11-09, $425M of them longs [17].

- Relative-value rotation: rotation flows toward BTC, Solana, Hyperliquid and others. ETH/BTC was 0.02798 at 14:3x UTC and 0.02770 at 15:41 UTC on 2026-11-10 (Etherscan ethprice [2]).

