- ether.fi $3.94B

- Activity versus value: Coin Metrics data (as reported) shows L1 averaging a record 2.16M transactions a day in 2026 and record active addresses [23]. Fees nonetheless collapsed, because capacity was raised (gas limit 30M → 60M, blobs 6 → 14 target) faster than demand. Ethereum chose cheap blockspace over high fees on purpose.

### Revenue and value capture

Mechanism (exact): there is no fee-collector contract.

- (a) Gross fees = base fee × gas used + priority fee × gas used + blob base fee × blob gas, paid by every transaction.

- (b) "Protocol revenue" / burn. The base-fee and blob-fee portions are never credited to anyone. The execution client simply does not mint them, so they leave supply. The function is EIP-1559 state-transition logic, not a contract call. Etherscan reports the cumulative figure as BurntFees.

- Priority fees are credited to the block's feeRecipient (miner field), which is set by the proposer or builder.

- (c) Value to holders:

- all holders receive the burn pro rata (supply reduction);

- stakers additionally receive issuance, tips and MEV.

Issuance is a transfer from non-stakers to stakers (dilution), not revenue.

| Period (ending 2026-11-09 UTC day, DefiLlama [6]) | (a) Gross fees | (b)=(c) Burn ("revenue" = "holders revenue") | Implied priority fees to validators (a−b) |

