#### History of fee collapse (DefiLlama calendar-year sums [6])

| Year | Fees | Burn |

| --- | --- | --- |

| 2021 (burn from August) | $9.91B | $5.14B |

| 2022 | $4.30B | $3.66B |

| 2023 | $2.41B | $2.02B |

| 2024 | $2.47B | $1.99B |

| 2025 | $523M | $285M |

| 2026 to 2026-11-09 | $152M | $43M (≈19–20k ETH, estimate) |

Stated plan to monetize: Ethereum has no "fee switch" to turn on. There are three official and semi-official positions:

- The scaling roadmap (60M → 200M+ gas, more blobs, Glamsterdam/Hegotá) aims to grow total fee volume by making blockspace abundant. Blob fees and the L1 base fee should rise if demand reaches the target. Today blob demand (9.9 blobs/block) is below the 14 target.

- Buterin's 2025 L2-alignment posts asked L2s to share fees with L1 through burn or staking [19b]. No major L2 has done so at scale.

- Buterin's 2026-11-08 post explicitly de-emphasises burn and frames ETH as a store-of-value and collateral asset [19].

Practical conclusion: value accrual to holders today is monetary demand plus staking, not cash flow.

### How to verify on-chain

None of the commands below need an API key except Etherscan. Store that key in an environment variable. All values quoted below were obtained by the author on 2026-11-10 between 14:30 and 14:45 UTC.

#### 1. Latest block: base fee, gas, blobs (public RPC)

curl -s https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com -H 'content-type: application/json' \ -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"eth_getBlockByNumber","params":["latest",false]}' \ | jq '.result | {number, baseFeePerGas, gasUsed, gasLimit, blobGasUsed, excessBlobGas}'

Obtained: block 26,385,129; baseFee 0.3704 gwei; gasUsed 35,661,479 of 60,000,000; blobGasUsed 1,703,936 (= 13 blobs × 131,072). Fact-check re-run (15:41 UTC): block 26,385,476; baseFee 2.3035 gwei; gasUsed 59,999,884 of 60,000,000; blobGasUsed 2,490,368 (= 19 blobs); eth_blobBaseFee = 0x7d295eb = 0.131 gwei. The burn of one block = baseFeePerGas × gasUsed + blobBaseFee × blobGasUsed. Get the blob base fee from eth_blobBaseFee or eth_feeHistory (baseFeePerBlobGas).

#### 2. Burn over a window (sum over blocks)

curl -s https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com -H 'content-type: application/json' \ -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"eth_feeHistory","params":["0x400","latest",[50]]}' \ | jq '.result | {oldestBlock, baseFeePerGas: .baseFeePerGas[0:3], gasUsedRatio: .gasUsedRatio[0:3], baseFeePerBlobGas: .baseFeePerBlobGas[0:3]}'# burn_i = baseFeePerGas_i * gasUsedRatio_i * gasLimit  (+ baseFeePerBlobGas_i * blobGasUsed_i)

Obtained: over the last 1,024 blocks, the average base fee was 0.1414 gwei and blocks were on average 51.2% full (fact-check re-run at 15:41 UTC: 0.345 gwei, 52.0% full). An exact per-block sum over blocks 26,384,839–26,385,138 (300 blocks, 1.01 h) gave 2.7901 ETH base-fee burn and 0.0083 ETH blob burn. Public RPCs rate-limit large batches. A 7,200-block (24 h) batch returned HTTP 429, so use ultrasound.money or your own node for day-scale sums.

#### 3. Supply, cumulative burn, staking rewards (Etherscan V2)

curl -s "https://api.etherscan.io/v2/api?chainid=1&module=stats&action=ethsupply2&apikey=$ETHERSCAN_API_KEY"# total supply = EthSupply + Eth2Staking - BurntFees  (all values in wei)

Obtained: EthSupply 122,373,866.22; Eth2Staking 4,476,259.23; BurntFees 4,643,279.94; WithdrawnTotal 7,618,584.35 ETH. Total = 122,206,845.5 ETH, which matches CoinGecko's 122,206,811 within 35 ETH. Fact-check re-run (~15:42 UTC): BurntFees 4,643,314.24 ETH, total 122,206,811.2 ETH, identical to CoinGecko's figure.

#### 4. Issuance, burn and supply (ultrasound.money public API)

curl -s https://ultrasound.money/api/v2/fees/burn-sums      # d1/d7/d30/since_burn in ETH and USDcurl -s https://ultrasound.money/api/v2/fees/gauge-rates    # yearly burn, issuance, supply growthcurl -s https://ultrasound.money/api/v2/fees/supply-over-timecurl -s https://ultrasound.money/api/v2/fees/issuance-estimate

Obtained:

- burn d30: 2,726.84 ETH;

- issuance_rate_yearly (d30): 1,089,633 ETH;

- burn_rate_yearly (d30): 33,201 ETH;

- supply_growth_rate_yearly: 0.8645%;

- issuance per slot: 0.41554 ETH.

#### 5. DefiLlama fees/revenue (independent aggregator)

curl -s "https://api.llama.fi/summary/fees/ethereum?dataType=dailyFees"     | jq '{total24h,total7d,total30d,total1y}'curl -s "https://api.llama.fi/summary/fees/ethereum?dataType=dailyRevenue"  | jq '{total24h,total7d,total30d,total1y}'curl -s "https://api.llama.fi/summary/fees/ethereum?dataType=dailyHoldersRevenue" | jq '.methodology'

#### 6. Staking: balances, queue, APR

# Beacon API (public): head and finalitycurl -s https://ethereum-beacon-api.publicnode.com/eth/v1/beacon/headers/headcurl -s https://ethereum-beacon-api.publicnode.com/eth/v1/beacon/states/head/finality_checkpoints# Sum of validator balances (gwei), as computed by ultrasound.moneycurl -s https://ultrasound.money/api/v2/fees/supply-parts   # beaconBalancesSum# Entry/exit queues and APR history: https://www.validatorqueue.com/ (data embedded in page)

Obtained: beaconBalancesSum = 44,583,639 ETH at slot 15,624,765. validatorqueue (2026-11-09): staked 44,489,256 ETH, 814,454 validators, entry queue 903,754 ETH (16.15 days), exit queue 5,061 ETH (1.12 days), APR 2.462%. Note that the deposit contract's ETH balance (93,255,027 ETH) is cumulative deposits, not current stake:

curl -s https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com -H 'content-type: application/json' \ -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"eth_getBalance","params":["0x00000000219ab540356cBB839Cbe05303d7705Fa","latest"]}'

#### 7. Key holder balances

# EF main wallet, WETH, Arbitrum bridge, Base portal (same eth_getBalance call, different address)0xde0B295669a9FD93d5F28D9Ec85E40f4cb697BAe   -> 4,774.55 ETH0xC02aaA39b223FE8D0A0e5C4F27eAD9083C756Cc2   -> 2,102,909 ETH (14:3x UTC); 2,098,356 ETH (15:4x UTC)0x8315177aB297bA92A06054cE80a67Ed4DBd7ed3a   -> 755,400 ETH0x49048044D57e1C92A77f79988d21Fa8fAF74E97e   -> 854,222 ETH (14:3x UTC); 855,943 ETH (15:4x UTC)

#### 8. MEV / builder concentration

https://www.relayscan.io/overview?t=7d. Obtained: Titan builder 52.1%, Quasar 17.7%, BuilderNet 15.2%. Top relay: ultrasound, 33.8% of payloads.

### Key people

Structure: there is no CEO of Ethereum. Three groups matter:

- Stiftung Ethereum (Ethereum Foundation, Zug, Switzerland). A non-profit that funds research, client teams and grants. In June 2026 it was reorganized into five "layer" clusters (protocol, access, user, community, institutional) plus operations and management, and headcount was cut by 54 people, about 20% [9].

- Independent client teams.

- Execution: Geth (EF), Nethermind, Besu, Erigon, Reth (Paradigm), Ethrex (LambdaClass).

- Consensus: Prysm (Offchain Labs), Lighthouse (Sigma Prime), Teku (Consensys), Nimbus (Status), Lodestar (ChainSafe), Grandine. The full client list is in the Hoodi release table [8].

- All Core Devs. The public calls that decide forks.

