- 2026-11-05: Protos reports a lawsuit by former EF Protocol Support lead Kayleen Fulton against Stiftung Ethereum, Vitalik Buterin and others. She alleges wrongful termination tied to opposition to the "Mandate" (including a "Source Seppuku License" clause) and a cancer diagnosis [25].

- 2026-11-06, updated 2026-11-09: the EF publishes a call to developers. ChainSecurity found deployed contracts that break under EIP-8037/8038, and four more reports have arrived. ACD may revise the specs, and the Hoodi date waits for audit results. Published timeline: Dedaub and ChainSecurity reports due 2026-11-13, findings presented on ACDT 2026-11-16, discussion on ACDE 2026-11-19 with no pre-committed decision [31].

- 2026-11-08: Buterin says ETH should be valued as a store of value. EIP-1559 stays, but burn need not be meaningful [19].

- 2026-11-09: about $462M of liquidations (about $425M longs). Spot ETFs see −$184.5M of outflows, net assets of $12.07B, and cumulative inflow of $11.74B. BitMine buys 9,587 ETH and reaches 6,070,092 [17].

- 2026-11-10: an EF-linked wallet sells 1,000 ETH (Lookonchain via [17]). ETH trades at $2,061 at 14:31 UTC and $2,037 at 15:39 UTC [1].

### Current problems and risks

- No fee-based value accrual.

- 30-day fees are $20.05M and burn is $6.21M.

- Tron out-earned Ethereum on 30-day fees ($24.5M), and Solana out-earned it over 365 days ($231.8M vs $176.5M) [6].

- Supply is inflating at +0.86% a year, and the "ultrasound money" narrative is effectively dead.

- Even Buterin now argues burn should not matter [19].

- L2s capture users but return little to L1.

- L2s run 25.6M transactions a day versus 1.84M on L1.

- They paid L1 only about $83.6k in the last 30 days [15].

- Base, Robinhood Chain and others are company-run chains, and several are still Stage 0 [22].

- Declining DeFi and stablecoin footprint.

- Ethereum DeFi TVL fell from $74.1B (2025-11-10) to $41.1B (2026-11-10) year on year. This is partly price, since ETH itself fell 42.5%.

- DefiLlama shows the stablecoin float on Ethereum fell from $166.7B (2025-11-10) to $135.3B (2026-11-10) year on year (stablecoincharts/Ethereum, re-queried by the fact-checker) [11]. Most of the drop came after May 2026 ($163.2B on 2026-05-01); causes (redemptions, chain migration, methodology) were not determined.

- Flow overhang.

- The ETFs are in persistent outflow [16][17].

- BitMine is nearly done buying and may sell staking rewards [18].

- Smaller treasury companies have sold [18].

- BitMine's single-entity concentration (≈5% of supply, 86% of it staked) is a tail risk if its equity premium collapses.

- Governance and organizational instability at the EF.

- Both co-EDs left in 2026.

- About 20% of staff were cut [9].

- A Mandate controversy led to a lawsuit [25].

- Van Epps warned that core devs could run short of funding: about $30M a year is needed, and the Client Incentive Program is winding down [29].

- Upgrade execution risk. Glamsterdam's state repricing breaks invariants in some immutable contracts. Its mainnet date is unknown and the spec may be revised [8][31].

- Centralization.

- One builder (Titan) builds 52% of MEV-Boost blocks, and the top three build about 85% [14].

- Lido holds about 22% of stake.

- Operator incidents (MetaMask Staking) can move hundreds of thousands of ETH into exit queues [24].

- Price and relative performance. ETH is −58.8% from its ATH, −42.5% year on year, −18.7% in 30 days (CoinGecko, 2026-11-10 15:39 UTC), and ETH/BTC is 0.0277 [1][2]. Market reports note ETH underperforming BTC in November [17].

- What the bulls still believe (evidence-based):

- record L1 transactions and active addresses [23];

- a persistent staking entry queue: 0.9–4.0M ETH waiting all year, 36.4% of supply staked, a record [5];

- dominant DeFi and stablecoin share (≈53% / 46%) [11];

- institutional rails via staking ETFs (ETHB) [20];

- a credible long-term roadmap: 200M gas target, zkEVM, post-quantum by 2029 [8][10].

