#### Genesis allocation and emissions

- Genesis allocation (Sept 2020): 60% community, which comprised a 15% retroactive airdrop to users and LPs, a 2% initial liquidity-mining program and 43% to the governance treasury vesting over 4 years. The rest went 21.27% to team, 18.04% to investors and 0.69% to advisors, all on 4-year vesting. All original vesting ended in September 2024, so there are no remaining cliff unlocks ([Uniswap blog, "Introducing UNI"](https://blog.uniswap.org/uni)).

- Ongoing supply out of the treasury, the "Growth Budget": UNIfication approved 20M UNI per year for Uniswap Labs, paid quarterly (5M UNI) through the UNIVesting contract 0xCa046A83EDB78F74aE338bb5A291bF6FdAc9e1D2. The treasury approved 40M UNI (two years) and has a remaining allowance of 20,000,000 UNI (on-chain allowance()). Four tranches of 5M UNI have been paid to recipient 0xaba63748c4b4def4a3319c3a29fe4829029d926f, on 2026-01-05, 2026-04-10, 2026-07-14 and 2026-10-01 (Etherscan token transfers). The recipient forwards each tranche to other addresses; the final destinations were not traced. lastUnlockTimestamp = 2026-10-01, so the next 5M UNI tranche becomes withdrawable about 2026-12-31.

- Other treasury movements in the last year: 12.5M UNI of 2022–23 "Franchiser" delegations were recalled back to the Timelock on 2026-06-01, after the vote in [thread 26099](https://gov.uniswap.org/t/temp-check-return-12-5m-delegated-tokens-to-the-governance-timelock/26099).

- Largest holders: the Timelock (262.2M) and 0xdead (113.9M) are the largest. Uniswap Foundation reported 15.1M UNI as of 2025-12-31 ([The Block, FY2025 report](https://www.theblock.co/post/396027/uniswap-foundation-projects-funding-runway-through-january-2027-as-treasury-reaches-85-8-million)). A full holder ranking was not obtained: Etherscan's holder-list endpoint requires a paid tier.

### How it works

#### The product (for users)

- AMM DEX: Uniswap is a set of immutable, non-custodial automated-market-maker contracts.

- Traders swap token A for token B against a pool. They pay an LP fee taken from the input amount.

- Liquidity providers (LPs) deposit token pairs and earn that fee. In exchange they take inventory risk: impermanent loss, or "LVR", the loss to arbitrageurs.

- Versions (all still live):

- v2 (2020): full-range pools, 0.30% fee.

- v3 (2021): concentrated liquidity, with fee tiers of 0.01%, 0.05%, 0.30% and 1%.

- v4 (2025): a singleton "PoolManager" (0x000000000004444c5dc75cB358380D2e3dE08A90 on Ethereum) with hooks, custom code attached to pools. Hooks enable dynamic fees, limit orders, launch auctions, permissioned pools for regulated assets, and "aggregator hooks" that wrap other DEXs' liquidity.

- UniswapX: an off-chain-signed, filler/solver-executed order flow.

- Uniswap Labs products are separate from the protocol: the web app, mobile wallet, Uniswap API, and the pools.trade launchpad on Robinhood Chain.

#### The fee switch and burn, introduced by UNIfication

- Approval: proposed 2025-11-10 by Hayden Adams (Labs), Ken Ng and Devin Walsh (Foundation). Approved by governance on 2025-12-26 (per the [UF FY2025 financial summary](https://gov.uniswap.org/t/uniswap-foundation-summary-fy-2025-financials/26068)); the 100M UNI burn was executed 2025-12-27 ([proposal](https://gov.uniswap.org/t/unification-proposal/25881), [The Block](https://www.theblock.co/post/383742/uniswap-passes-unification-proposal)).

- Fee levels:

- v2: the protocol takes 0.05% of the 0.30% fee, leaving LPs 0.25%. The fee accrues as newly minted LP tokens sent to feeTo.

- v3: the protocol takes 1/4 of the LP fee on 0.01% and 0.05% pools, and 1/6 on 0.30% and 1% pools.

- v4 (since July 2026): a curve. A 5 bp LP fee gives 1.25 bp to the protocol, 30 bp gives 5 bp, and the cap is 10 bp. It applies to hookless pools and pools launched via Continuous Clearing Auction. Aggregator-hook pools pay 10 bp (3 bp on stable pairs), and on Base 3 bp (1 bp) ([v4 fee thread](https://gov.uniswap.org/t/temp-check-activate-v4-protocol-fees/26162)).

- Contracts per chain: a fee adapter for each version pushes fees into one immutable TokenJar per chain. The only way to withdraw from the TokenJar is the Releaser. On Ethereum this is the Firepit: release(nonce, assets[], recipient) pulls threshold UNI from the caller and sends it to 0xdead, then transfers the selected assets from the TokenJar to the caller.

- Ethereum threshold = 4,000 UNI per release; Firepit nonce = 1,546 releases (15:42 UTC; 1,545 at 14:33 UTC).

- Base threshold = 2,000 UNI; Base nonce = 1,611 (15:42 UTC).

- Searchers call release() when the jar's contents are worth more than the UNI they must burn, so burns track fees in near-real time. A searcher margin and price effects apply.

- Cross-chain burns: on L2s and alt-L1s, the releasers bridge the UNI back to Ethereum and deliver it to 0xdead.

- OP Stack chains use OptimismBridgedResourceFirepit; Arbitrum and Orbit chains use ArbitrumBridgedResourceFirepit / ArbitrumOrbitResourceFirepit.

- BNB, Polygon and Arc use Wormhole NTT burn (WormholeReleaser).

- Canonical OP/Arbitrum withdrawals take about 7 days to finalize, so L2 burns show up at 0xdead with a lag.

- Where fees are on now:

- v2 and v3: all pools on Ethereum, Arbitrum, Base, Celo, OP Mainnet, Soneium, X Layer, World Chain, Zora, BNB Chain, Polygon, Robinhood Chain and Arc.

- v4: selected families on those chains.

- Unichain: sequencer fees route to the burn.

- Not yet fee-enabled (per forum threads): other v4 families, UniswapX, the Protocol Fee Discount Auction (PFDA), and chains such as Avalanche and Monad.

- Governance: UNI holders delegate and vote via Snapshot, then GovernorBravo, then the Timelock.

- Since UNIfication, fee-parameter proposals can skip the RFC stage: a 5-day Snapshot followed by an on-chain vote.

- The DAO has a legal wrapper, DUNI (a Wyoming DUNA adopted in 2025), which signs the services agreement with Uniswap Labs and files taxes.

- There is no staking, no ve-locking and no fee distribution to UNI holders. UNI is not used for gas: Unichain gas is ETH.

### Utility

- Real, protocol-enforced:

- Claim on protocol fees via burn. UNI is the only asset accepted to unlock the TokenJars. Every dollar of protocol fees therefore creates roughly a dollar of UNI demand that is then destroyed.

- Governance. UNI holders control a 262M UNI treasury, fee levels per pool family and chain, the Growth Budget, deployments to new chains, and the owner roles of v2/v3 factories, v4 PoolManagers and governance-owned hooks (e.g. VolatilePair hook).

- Secondary or external: collateral in lending markets (e.g. Aave, Compound), and index/ETP inclusion.

- NOT needed for: swapping, providing liquidity or paying fees on Uniswap (users pay in the traded tokens); gas on Unichain (ETH); or using the Uniswap app or API (Labs interface/API fees were set to 0 in Dec 2025). Holders receive no cash flow directly. Value accrues only through supply reduction.

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- Fee-funded burn ("real yield" narrative). The fee-funded burn is about $8.3M per 30 days, about $100M a year at the 30-day pace, or $141M a year at the 90-day pace. At a $4.18B market cap (2026-11-10), that is a market-cap-to-annualized-burn multiple of about 42x (30d) or about 30x (90d), computed by the author. The market treats the burn as a lever on DEX activity.

- Activity spikes. The September 2026 Robinhood Chain memecoin and stock-token boom pushed Uniswap fees to $204M in that month (DL). UNI rallied from $3.28 (2026-08-18) to $10.22 (2026-09-23, daily closes, CG). Press attributed the move to Robinhood Chain volume and record burns ([CryptoPotato](https://cryptopotato.com/uni-jumps-16-as-robinhood-chain-dex-volume-hits-1-3b/)).

- Tokenization / institutional narrative. BlackRock BUIDL via UniswapX with Securitize (2026-02-11); tokenized equities in Uniswap apps (2026-06-12); Permissioned Pools hook (2026-07-23); Circle's Arc (2026-09-16); SMBC Nikko / Nethermind Japan venue (announced 2026-10-02, launch mid-2027).

- Large "blue-chip DeFi" liquidity on most CEXs, with derivatives listed.

#### Supply / sell pressure

- Growth Budget: 20M UNI per year to Labs (5M per quarter), about $137M a year at $6.87. This is larger than the current fee burn in both UNI terms (about 13.9M UNI burned in the past ~10.5 months, versus 20M distributed in 2026) and USD terms. Net of fee burns, about +6.1M UNI has been added to non-treasury supply in 2026 to date (20M distributed minus 13.9M burned; a delegate made the same point in [thread 26162](https://gov.uniswap.org/t/temp-check-activate-v4-protocol-fees/26162) on 2026-07-16), i.e. net inflationary to the float. This ignores the 100M retro burn, which came from the treasury.

- Treasury overhang: 262M UNI in the Timelock, plus the dormant 2% per year mint right.

- Foundation holdings (15.1M UNI at end-2025) are being spent as grants.

- Index removal: Bitwise dropped UNI from the Bitwise 10 Large Cap index and its ex-BTC version at the 2026-10-30 rebalance, replacing it with NEAR ([Bitwise notice](https://bitwiseinvestments.com/indexes/rebalance-notifications/bitwise-crypto-asset-indexes)).

- Searchers must buy UNI to burn, which is demand, but they sell the received jar assets. This flow is neutral for those assets and positive for UNI.

### The protocol/business

- What it does: the largest on-chain spot exchange protocol, deployed on about 40 chains. Lifetime volume is about $4.02T (DL, all-time, to 2026-11-10) and lifetime LP fees about $6.15B (DL).

- Product lines:

- Protocol: v2, v3 and v4 plus hooks; UniswapX; Unichain, an OP-Stack L2 launched Feb 2025 and moved to "OP Enterprise" operations (testnet 2026-10-13, mainnet 2026-10-29, per [Uniswap blog](https://blog.uniswap.org/latest-updates)).

- Labs products: web app, wallet, API (now zero-margin), Earn (Morpho-powered, from 2026-07-30), the pools.trade launchpad (2026-08-05), developer analytics (2026-10-28), and TJAR, the TokenJar/Releaser design offered as fee-conversion infrastructure to other apps, with Robinhood the first integrator (2026-10-29, [The Block](https://theblock.co/news/defi/2026-10-29-uniswap-token-jar-tjar-robinhood-432131)).

- Audience:

- Retail DeFi traders via the app and wallet.

- Aggregators and solvers (1inch, CoW, 0x, UniswapX fillers) routing into Uniswap pools.

- Professional LPs and market makers.

- Token issuers and launchpads.

- Increasingly, RWA issuers and brokerages: Robinhood, Securitize, SMBC Nikko.

- Where it is popular: by LP fees over the 30 days 2026-10-11 to 2026-11-09 (DL fees totalDataChartBreakdown, re-aggregated 2026-11-10):

