- BNB, Polygon and Celo added (May 2026).

- Robinhood Chain fees and v4 fees (Snapshot July 7–12; on-chain proposals #99/#100; v4 executed about 2026-07-27, per [Crypto Briefing](https://cryptobriefing.com/uniswap-fee-switch-v4-pools-325k-revenue/)).

- 12.5M UNI of Franchiser delegations recalled to the treasury (2026-06-01).

### Current problems and risks

- Fee concentration in one speculative venue. Robinhood Chain generated 37.2% of Uniswap's last-30-day LP fees and about 2/3 of September's ($140M of $204M), driven by memecoin launches and stock-token trading. It is already fading: fees fell from $204M in September to $92M in October, and November 1–9 ($17.7M) extrapolates to about $59M per 30 days. The burn halved in the same period: $17.8M in September, $9.0M in October, about $2.27M in November 1–10 (day 10 partial).

- Net emission versus burn. The Growth Budget releases 20M UNI per year to Labs, more than the roughly 14M UNI per year that fees currently burn. Without the one-off 100M retro burn, which came from the treasury and was not circulating, UNIfication is so far a net transfer from treasury to Labs larger than the fee burn.

- Low holder take rate and LP pushback. Only about 10% of LP fees reach holders. Raising the take risks LP migration to Aero (Aerodrome/Velodrome), PancakeSwap, Fluid or to v3 from v4. An LP with $500K publicly objected to the v4 fee (forum, 2026-07-22). Labs reported (2026-07-18) that the 25 largest Ethereum v3 pools kept 98.5% of their pre-activation liquidity in token terms after the v3 fee switch, and that their swap volume fell 20% against a 62% decline in total Ethereum DEX volume ([thread 26162](https://gov.uniswap.org/t/temp-check-activate-v4-protocol-fees/26162)).

- Volume trend. Monthly Uniswap volume fell from $123.6B (Oct 2025) to about $40B per month (Mar–May 2026) before the Robinhood-led rebound. Aggregators and solvers, as well as CEX and Hyperliquid spot, compete for flow. Aero's (Aerodrome + Velodrome + Aero multichain deployments) holder revenue ($7.3M per 30 days) is close to Uniswap's on about 31% of the volume, and Aero is now also deployed on Robinhood Chain, Ethereum and Arc.

- Unichain underdelivered. TVL is about $20.4M, Unichain-attributed holders revenue is $770 per 30 days, and operations have been handed to OP Enterprise. UNIfication had cited about $7.5M per year of sequencer fees.

- Complexity and security surface. Burns depend on many adapters, bridges (OP/Arbitrum canonical bridges, Wormhole NTT) and cross-chain governance paths. A Celo activation failed once due to a configuration error (thread 26108). A mainnet fee adapter deployment had to be redeployed because its default fee was 0 (thread 26035). The governance-owned upgradeable hooks (StablePair, VolatilePair) add upgrade-key risk held by governance.

- Governance centralization around Labs. The Foundation has been folded into Labs, Labs drafts nearly all proposals, and Labs is paid in UNI. Labs says turnout is healthy: passed proposals since DUNI averaged about 75M votes, about 88% above the 40M quorum (thread 26099, 2026-04-24). Delegates SEEDGov and kfx replied that only Labs- or Foundation-backed proposals reach quorum (same thread, 2026-05-06).

- Market and index factors. The Bitwise index exit, a broad "Fear" market (Crypto Fear & Greed Index 32 on 2026-11-10, 25–37 on 2026-11-01..09 except 50 on 11-05, per [alternative.me](https://api.alternative.me/fng/?limit=10)), and UNI down about 33% from its 2026-09-23 daily-close high.

- Regulatory. The US backdrop improved: the SEC closed its Uniswap Labs investigation in Feb 2025 (after a 2024 Wells notice; widely reported, not re-verified here), and DUNI gives the DAO a legal form. Open questions remain on EU MiCA treatment of front-ends ([thread 26137](https://gov.uniswap.org/t/discussion-privacy-preserving-mica-compliance-how-is-uniswap-handling-eu-users/26137)) and on tokenized-equity pools (weekend price dislocations on Robinhood Chain stock tokens, thread 26297).

- What the market believes in (bull case): Uniswap as the default venue for tokenized assets (Robinhood, Circle/Arc, BlackRock BUIDL, SMBC Nikko); v4 hooks and aggregator hooks capturing fees even on external liquidity; and fee coverage expanding to more chains, pool families, UniswapX and PFDA, so that the burn scales with any activity boom.

### What would move the token

- About 2026-11-13: Snapshot on the VolatilePair hook upgrade and X Layer releaser. Routine; neutral.

- About 2026-12-31 / Jan 2027: next 5M UNI Growth-Budget tranche (the last under the current 40M approval runs to end-2027). Supply event, mildly bearish if sold.

- Pending / possible governance: v4 fees on remaining families and chains (e.g. Elysium, Monad, Avalanche, Unichain v4); UniswapX fees; PFDA launch; growth of aggregator-hook volume (already fee-enabled). Each adds burn and is bullish if LP liquidity holds.

- Activity regime: another Robinhood Chain or memecoin or stock-token boom (bullish: September showed about $18M per month of burn) versus fading volume (bearish: burn reverts to the H1 2026 run-rate of $3–5M per month).

- Institutional / RWA: volumes from Arc, BUIDL/Securitize, Robinhood TJAR, and the SMBC Nikko Japan venue (mid-2027).

- Bearish risks: a bridge or adapter exploit affecting TokenJars; governance approving use of the 2% mint or a larger Growth Budget; LP migration after fee increases; more index removals.

- Metrics to track weekly: UNI inflow to 0xdead (UNI and USD); DL holders revenue; Robinhood Chain share of fees; Uniswap's share of DEX volume; and Timelock outflows.

