Three liquid-locker protocols, Convex, StakeDAO and Yearn, together control about 80% of veCRV voting power. Convex alone has a majority, so in practice Convex governance (vlCVX holders) and its vote-incentive markets direct most CRV emissions and most DAO votes. Egorov's address is the one commonly attributed to him on explorers; he may hold more elsewhere.

### How it works

#### The product

- StableSwap pools. This AMM concentrates liquidity around a 1:1 price, giving very low slippage for like-kind assets (USDC/USDT, stETH/ETH, WBTC/cbBTC). The amplification parameter "A" sets the degree of concentration. Typical stable-pool fees are 0.01%–0.04%. StableSwap-NG is the current factory, and more than 1,000 Stableswap-NG pools now exist on Ethereum [7].

- CryptoSwap / Tricrypto / TwoCrypto-NG pools. These are for volatile pairs such as USDT/WBTC/WETH. Liquidity concentrates around an internal oracle price that re-pegs over time, and fees are dynamic.

- FXSwap. A pool design for currency pairs such as USD/EUR, USD/CHF or USD/BRL. It uses a moving price reference with arbitrage-driven rebalancing. Curve reports more than 20 active FX pools across 10 currency corridors on 5 networks [11].

- crvUSD. Curve's over-collateralized stablecoin. Users mint it against BTC and ETH collateral in "mint markets" that use LLAMMA, a lending AMM that converts collateral to crvUSD gradually as price falls ("soft liquidation") instead of in one hard liquidation. PegKeepers mint or withdraw crvUSD into stable pools to hold the peg, and a monetary policy sets borrow rates from PegKeeper debt. scrvUSD is the savings vault that earns part of borrow interest [5][6].

- Llamalend (V1 and V2). Isolated lending markets built on LLAMMA. V2 launched on Optimism in June 2026 and on Ethereum mainnet in July 2026 [7][16]. Unlike V1, it does not require crvUSD on either side, accepts Curve LP tokens and yield-bearing vault tokens as collateral, opens each market with a borrow cap of zero until a governance vote raises it, and charges a 10% admin fee to the DAO [7].

#### The token mechanics

- Vote-escrow (ve) locking. Lock CRV for up to 4 years to receive veCRV. 1 CRV locked for 4 years gives 1 veCRV, and the balance decays linearly to zero at unlock. Locked CRV cannot be withdrawn early [9][10].

- Fee share. veCRV holders receive the DAO's cut of protocol fees, paid weekly in crvUSD through the FeeDistributor 0xD16d5eC345Dd86Fb63C6a9C43c517210F1027914. A FeeAllocator (deployed 2025-06-20, first distribution 2025-07-02, on-chain) has routed 10% of these fees to the DAO treasury since then. The pipeline is described in section 7 [12].

- Gauge voting. Each week, veCRV holders vote on how the 1.86M CRV of emissions is split across pools and lending markets. That is why third parties pay "vote incentives" (bribes) on Votium and Votemarket: the 2-week average was $258.6k per week as of 2026-11-05 [6]. Most bribes go to Convex vlCVX voters, not directly to plain veCRV lockers.

- Boost. An LP who also holds veCRV earns up to 2.5x more CRV emissions on their pool positions. Convex, StakeDAO and Yearn pool their veCRV to offer the boost to LPs who hold no veCRV, which is how they came to dominate veCRV.

- Governance. All binding votes run through Aragon on Ethereum. On-chain parameters of the Ownership voting app 0xE478de485ad2fe566d49342Cbd03E49ed7DB3356 are a 7-day vote (voteTime() = 604,800 s), 30% quorum (minAcceptQuorumPct = 0.30) and 51% support (supportRequiredPct = 0.51). Creating a vote takes 2,500 veCRV [9]. Votes numbered 1,523 as of 2026-11-10 (votesLength()). An Emergency DAO multisig can pause and de-risk certain components, and since vote 1510 (Oct 2026) this covers Llamalend V2 on 5 chains [6].

- No buyback, no burn. CRV has no buyback or burn mechanism. Value reaches holders only as crvUSD distributions to veCRV, plus governance control over emissions.

### Utility

- Real utility, requires locking: (1) a share of protocol fees, paid in crvUSD; (2) gauge votes that direct about $471k a week of CRV emissions at today's price (1.863M CRV × $0.2529), which can be sold to bribe markets; (3) up to 2.5x boost on LP rewards; (4) binding DAO governance over parameters, gauges, crvUSD debt ceilings, treasury and grants.

- Utility of liquid CRV: collateral in Llamalend and other lenders, and trading or perps (CRV is listed on major CEXs and perp venues, including Binance, Coinbase, Upbit and Hyperliquid) [7].

- What CRV is NOT needed for: paying swap fees or gas, minting crvUSD (which takes BTC or ETH collateral), lending, or using any Curve product as a trader, LP or borrower. Users of Curve never need to hold CRV.

- Assessment: this is more than a governance-only token. veCRV gets an on-chain, verifiable stream of stablecoins. But the yield is low (about 3% a year on locked value over the last 52 weeks, rising to 6.6% in the volatile week to 2026-11-05) and below the 4% inflation rate, so non-lockers are diluted.

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- Fee yield and vote-incentive yield. veCRV APR was 6.58% from fees and 14.8% including vote incentives in the week to 2026-11-05. The prior week was much lower: fee APR 1.9% [6].

- Control of emissions. Stablecoin and LST issuers (Frax, Resupply, Inverse, Avant, Techdollar and others) buy or rent veCRV and vlCVX to direct CRV to their pools. This is the "Curve Wars" demand, now much smaller in USD terms than in 2021–22.

- Speculation and derivatives. On 2026-09-29 CRV rose 21.09% in 24 hours to $0.3984; the cited report gives no cause and no fundamental catalyst was found [19]. Current perp open interest is 68.5M CRV on Binance (about $17.3M) and 44.7M CRV on Hyperliquid (about $11.3M; public APIs, 2026-11-10 15:47 UTC).

- Narratives: Curve as the "home of stablecoins" (stablecoin issuers need deep pegged liquidity), on-chain FX, stablecoin L1s (Arc by Circle; Stellar), Yield Basis (BTC liquidity without impermanent loss, built on Curve and crvUSD), Llamalend V2 LP-as-collateral.

- Listings: Upbit added CRV/KRW and CRV/USDT in August 2026 [7]. The Korean retail market has historically been a source of CRV volume spikes.

#### Supply and sell pressure

- Emissions: 1.86M CRV a week, about $471k a week or $24.6M a year at $0.2529. Much of it is farmed and sold by LPs. The amount is about 3.6x the fees paid to veCRV over the last 52 weeks ($6.8M).

- Grant vesting: the Swiss Stake 17.45M CRV for 2026 must partly be sold to pay operating costs (about CHF 5.3M a year) [14][15]. Smaller grants go to yRisk and the Grants multisig.

- Liquid-locker discounts: cvxCRV, sdCRV and yCRV trade on secondary markets, sometimes at a discount to CRV, which gives an exit for locked CRV and caps the premium for locking.

- Founder-collateral overhang (historical): In June 2024 Egorov's CRV-backed loans were liquidated and left about $10M of bad debt in Llamalend, which he repaid within days. Egorov's current lock is 27.2M CRV until 2030 (on-chain). No large CRV-collateralized founder loans were identified in this research, but this was not exhaustively checked [17].

- Sector rotation: TVL fell 46% and DefiLlama-counted fees 43% year on year as capital moved toward other venues (section 6). The October 29, 2026 Binance hack (about $6.36B stolen per TRM Labs) hit altcoin sentiment broadly. CRV fell from $0.337 (Oct 29) to $0.251 (Nov 6) [18][2].

### The protocol/business

#### What it does and who uses it

- Core: Curve is the main on-chain venue for swapping like-kind assets and the default liquidity venue for new stablecoins. Issuers add pools, request gauges and pay vote incentives to bootstrap liquidity. Recent gauge requests include Techdollar USDte, Avant avUSD, Vetro VUSD, Alchemix alUSD, Resupply reUSD, 3Jane USD3, Mezo MUSD, Flying Tulip ftUSD and Frax frxUSD [6][15].

- Audience: stablecoin and LST/LRT issuers, DEX aggregators (most retail flow arrives through 1inch, CowSwap, Odos-type routers rather than the Curve UI), DAO treasuries and "yield farmers" via Convex/StakeDAO/Yearn, leveraged loopers on Llamalend V2 (sDOLA, sfrxUSD, syrupUSDC, sreUSD), BTC holders through Yield Basis.

- Where it is popular: Ethereum is overwhelmingly dominant. Over the last 365 days it produced $51.4M of the $58.8M in DefiLlama-counted fees (87.5%). Base ($2.19M), Plasma ($2.01M), Monad ($1.79M) and Robinhood Chain ($0.49M) follow [3]. Curve is deployed on 20+ EVM chains, plus Stellar (Soroban, first non-EVM, 2026-10-29) and Arc (Circle's L1, 2026-09-17) [11][7]. Regionally, the new FX pools target LatAm (BRAt/ARGt via Twin Finance on Arbitrum) and Switzerland (ZCHF/svZCHF from Frankencoin) [7].

